# 08 — Structs, Enums & Methods

## What you will learn

Structs model data with named fields. Enums model values that can have one of several variants. Methods attach behavior to a type.

## Structs

```rust
struct User {
    name: String,
    age: u32,
}
```

Create one:

```rust
let user = User {
    name: String::from("Ndk"),
    age: 21,
};
```

## Enums

```rust
enum Status {
    Active,
    Disabled,
}
```

Enums are especially useful when a value has a finite set of meaningful states.

## Methods

Methods are defined in an `impl` block:

```rust
impl User {
    fn is_adult(&self) -> bool {
        self.age >= 18
    }
}
```

`&self` means the method borrows the instance immutably.

## Associated Functions

A function without `self` is often used as a constructor:

```rust
impl User {
    fn new(name: String, age: u32) -> Self {
        Self { name, age }
    }
}
```

## Practice

Create a `User` struct with a constructor and methods, plus a `Status` enum. Write behavior that converts the status into a string.

## Common Mistakes

- Confusing methods with associated functions.
- Forgetting `&self`, `&mut self`, or `self` has different ownership implications.
- Using strings where an enum would model a finite state more safely.

