# 11 — Traits, Generics & Trait Bounds

## Generics

Generics let one implementation work with multiple types:

```rust
fn identity<T>(value: T) -> T {
    value
}
```

`T` is a type parameter selected by the caller.

## Traits

Traits define behavior:

```rust
trait Summary {
    fn summary(&self) -> String;
}
```

A type implements a trait with `impl`:

```rust
impl Summary for User {
    fn summary(&self) -> String {
        format!("User: {}", self.name)
    }
}
```

## Trait Bounds

A generic function can require behavior:

```rust
fn print_summary<T: Summary>(item: &T) {
    println!("{}", item.summary());
}
```

This tells the compiler that every accepted `T` implements `Summary`.

For more complex signatures, use `where`:

```rust
fn print<T>(item: &T)
where
    T: Summary,
{
    println!("{}", item.summary());
}
```

## Associated Types

Traits can define a related type:

```rust
trait Container {
    type Item;
    fn get(&self) -> Self::Item;
}
```

The implementation chooses `Item`.

## Practice

Create a `Summary` trait, implement it for two types, and write a generic function that accepts any `Summary`.

## Common Mistakes

- Adding generic parameters without a real need.
- Confusing a trait with a concrete struct.
- Forgetting required trait bounds.

